[Music]  hi everyone I'm anne-marie green and I'm  vladimir duthiers thanks for joining us  bloodshed and burials are overshadowing  the new US Embassy in Jerusalem new  protests erupted along the Gaza border  and in the West Bank today Palestinian  demonstrators burned tires and threw  rocks at Israeli soldiers and police  this comes after yesterday's staggering  wave of violence nearly 60 people were  killed and thousands were injured in  deadly protests Holly Williams takes a  closer look at the volatile situation  and we want to warn you that some of the  video can be graphic for some viewers  they carried their dead to be buried  here in Gaza including several children  caught up in the bloodshed more than  1300 people are alive but suffered  gunshot wounds according to Palestinian  officials most of the dead were killed  along the border fence that separates  the tiny poverty-stricken gaza strip  from israel where tens of thousands of  Palestinians held angry demonstrations  yesterday furious after the US Embassy  was moved to Jerusalem Israel responded  with deadly force live ammunition aimed  at those it says got too close to the  fence the Palestinians hope East  Jerusalem will one day be their capital  if they ever get their own state and  they are furious over what they say is  American bias towards Israel against the  Hakam Abu shanab is a financial analyst  and was one of the protesters are you  willing to die here we don't have any  other method that we could use instead  of you know dying is the only way the  Israeli military says it also launched  airstrikes yesterday targeting a camp  run by Hamas the militant group that  controls the Gaza Strip and Israeli  media is reporting that Israel has  threatened to assassinate Hamas leaders  if the violent protests can  and CBS News foreign correspondent Holly  Williams is joining us now from Gaza  Holly take us through the scene today in  the aftermath of yesterday's violence  yeah I think that people here in the  Gaza Strip are quite shocked by the  bloodshed that they saw yesterday I mean  they've been holding these protests now  along the fence that separates Gaza from  Israel for six weeks and they'd been  loss of life up until yesterday around  40 people had been killed Israel had  been criticized for in a quote excessive  use of force  tens of thousands of people went to the  fence yesterday huge numbers but I don't  think anyone was ready to see so many  people dying the death toll according to  officials here is now more than 60 that  includes several children and also an  eight month-old baby a little girl layla  al gondor who died of tear gas  inhalation she was buried today and just  a few minutes earlier we saw two  Palestinian men who were killed  yesterday buried here today at martyrs  Cemetery in the Gaza Strip now there  were more protests planned for today to  mark the 70th anniversary of what  Palestinians called their day of  catastrophe following the founding of  the State of Israel but so far following  the violence yesterday very few  demonstrators have gone up to the border  fence so Holly you've talked to some of  the Palestinians out there a protesting  earlier this is video from yesterday  earlier we were showing what was  happening in Ramallah today they're a  long way from Jerusalem where the US  Embassy was opening up what is their  endgame here what are they hoping to  accomplish yeah I mean I think the  Palestinians generally and specifically  who live here in the Gaza Strip want a  lot of things they want their own state  they want the capital of their their  state to be East Jerusalem which is part  of the reason that they are so furious  about the u.s. moving its embassy to  Jerusalem these protests initially when  they began about six weeks ago were  terms the great march of return and the  idea was that Palestinians who live here  in Gaza want to return to lands that  their families fled in 1948 after the  founding of the State of Israel there  are about two million people living here  in Gaza and two-thirds of them are  either refugees or their descendants I  think there are more general  frustrations as well specifically here  in guards are where they've you know  they've experienced a blockade now for  more than ten years and they have a  whole host of problems including  contaminated water power cuts really  high unemployment at nearly 50% and  shortages of goods I think the problem  though is that these protests are  certainly an opportunity for  Palestinians to vent their frustration  but arguably they don't really do  anything for the Palestinians I mean  most of the Palestinian protesters are  peaceful those that are violent are  largely armed with rocks and slingshots  and a burning tires now they don't  inflict any serious damage on Israel and  they don't necessarily sway world  opinion and arguably they only serve to  alienate opinion in Israel even further  Holly yesterday the White House deputy  spokesperson Raj Shah called the  responsibility for violence he said it  rested squarely uploading him now  squarely with Hamas even though nearly  most of the Palestinians that have been  killed over the last couple of weeks  have been killed through Israeli live  fire launching air strikes as you've  seen over the last couple of days  explain the significance of Hamas we had  a spokesperson from the Israeli  government here on cbsn yesterday she  very much said the same thing that Raj  Shah said she also said that the that  Hamas does not provide any services for  people living in the Gaza Strip but back  in 2007 when they won the special  elections against the secular  photography they were promising people  that they were going to make their lives  better have they  I think it's been very difficult for  them to make people's lives better here  when the Gaza Strip has been suffering  the effects of this blockade I mean to  be clear Hamas is an Islamist militant  group that's been in power here in the  Gaza Strip since 2007 they weren't the  original instigators of these protests  as we understand it but they certainly  have encouraged them they've certainly  supported them and I suppose some would  argue that they've done so cynically  because they think that they if they  encourage Palestinians to go and protest  at the border fence some of them will be  killed by Israelis and that might go  some way to swaying public opinion in  their favor but remember also that  though Israel says that it has been  acting in self-defense others have  criticized it for what they see as an  excessive use of force and in fact the  UN has criticized what happened  yesterday as appalling deadly violence  Polly Williams thank you so much

